1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,240
Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem.
教育工作者和企业领导者的共同点，比人们可能以为的要多。

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,140
Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future.
教师希望让学生为成功的未来做好准备。

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:21,120
Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry.
科技公司有意培养一支劳动力队伍，使其具备发展壮大公司、推动行业进步所需的STEM（科学、技术、工程和数学）技能。

4
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,460
How can they work together to achieve these goals?
双方如何才能携手实现这些目标呢？

5
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:26,530
Play may be the answer.
玩耍或许就是答案。

6
00:00:27,330 --> 00:00:37,480
Focusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities.
注重STEM技能固然重要，但现实情况是，STEM技能与传统的、亲自动手的创造性活动结合起来时，会得到增强，也更有实际意义。

7
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:44,890
This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today’s children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow.
事实日益证明，要让如今的孩子做好准备，成为未来的创客和建造者，这种结合是最好的办法。

8
00:00:45,690 --> 00:00:51,530
That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good old-fashioned play.
正因如此，科技公司正与教育工作者合作，让传统的玩耍方式重新回归。

9
00:00:52,330 --> 00:01:11,500
In fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren’t related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity: skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks.
事实上，许多专家认为，21世纪最重要的技能与具体的技术或学科内容无关，而与创造力有关，这类技能包括想象力、发现问题和解决问题的能力、团队合作、乐观、耐心，以及尝试和冒险的能力。

10
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,650
These are skills acquired when kids tinker.
这些是孩子们在鼓捣小玩意时习得的技能。

11
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:24,500
High-tech organizations such as NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.
美国国家航空航天局喷气推进实验室等高科技机构发现，机构中解决问题综合能力最强的人，年轻时曾是鼓捣小玩意的高手。

12
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:33,730
There are cognitive benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again.
像我们小时候那样做事，对认知有好处：搭建一样东西，把它拆掉，然后再重新搭起来。

13
00:01:34,530 --> 00:01:45,200
Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities.
研究表明，如果给四五岁的孩子15分钟的自由玩耍时间，他们会用其中三分之一的时间从事空间、数学和建筑类活动。

14
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,790
This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.
这类玩耍，尤其是搭积木，有助于孩子发现并逐步掌握数学和几何中的关键原理。

15
00:01:54,590 --> 00:02:13,750
If play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that’s really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older.
如果玩耍和搭建对21世纪技能的发展至关重要，那么这真是个好消息，原因有二：孩子天生就是建造者、创客和创造者，因此，培养21世纪技能或许简单到只需给孩子留出空间，让他们去玩耍、鼓捣、尝试各种东西就行，哪怕他们渐渐长大也依然如此。

16
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:20,100
Secondly, it doesn’t take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills.
其次，培养21世纪技能并不需要21世纪的技术。

17
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:24,770
This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities.
这对资源不足的学校和社区来说尤为重要。

18
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:32,620
Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important “maker” skills.
手边有什么材料就拿来鼓捣一番，是调动那些重要“创客”技能的一种简单方法。

19
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:35,910
And anyone, anywhere, can do it.
而且无论是谁、无论在哪里，都能做到。
